#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084125} :activity/id #uuid "28dce159-b20f-5270-a0a2-505427e84a1b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048340 :topic/id #uuid "97021ff6-453f-5638-924e-46e41b2ed833" :topic/math-academy-id 711} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a student takes a test and gets a score of $x=82.$ If we know that the mean score for the class is $\\overline{x} = 75$ and the standard deviation is $\\sigma = 3.5,$ then how good is the student's score? \n\nTo determine how good the student's score is, we can calculate the **$z$-score**, which is defined by\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{x-\\overline{x}}{\\sigma}.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations the observed score is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\n- A large positive $z$-score means the student's score is high compared to the class's mean score.\n\n- A $z$-score close to $0$ means the student's score is close to the class's mean score.\n\n- A large negative $z$-score means the student's score is low compared to the class's mean score.\n\nIn this particular example, the student's $z$-score is\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{82 - 75}{3.5} = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the student's score is $2$ standard deviations above the mean. This suggests that the student obtained a much higher score than the average score for the entire class." :tutorial/id #uuid "79fbe8d7-9dd2-5396-a697-e06d76e71c6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "75c61202-a9c8-5b14-884c-0c243f39aa42" :step/math-academy-id 20613 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083026 :question/id #uuid "3a18e6a6-e190-5977-9e1f-0d115b16c9fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc94085c-4088-5eaf-887a-eea7f040573c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410438}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Z-Score of a Given Observation"} :step/id #uuid "c1a68bf5-c5d5-50ba-aa47-bafe92cbd183" :step/math-academy-id 20614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083027 :question/id #uuid "1c03d476-51f5-5dc4-bed6-04986f318924" :question/math-academy-id "e-5705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2169053d-78fc-5714-82f4-d89fc36ff695" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575587}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Observation, Standard Deviation, or Mean Given a Z-Score"} :step/id #uuid "b0edd80f-a5ee-5023-80d1-fdad43bc6b63" :step/math-academy-id 20616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084454 :tutorial/content "So far, we have practiced converting an observation $x$ into a $z$-score. Other times, though, we may be given a $z$-score and want to find the corresponding observation $x.$\n\nTo accomplish this, we can take the formula for the $z$-score and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\ z\\cdot \\sigma  & =x-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\ x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a data set has a mean of $\\overline{x}=15$ and a standard deviation of $\\sigma = 1.5.$ If we're given a $z$-score of $z=2,$ then we can find the corresponding observation $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =15+2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =15+3 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2772228-1477-5aed-b3ec-02d21f685274" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3705 :tutorial/title "Finding an Observation Corresponding to a Given Z-Score"} :step/id #uuid "56695f9f-5daf-5cc8-9b02-6106e78ba5bb" :step/math-academy-id 20707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083124 :question/id #uuid "68d14643-0faf-5cff-b4b9-cdb903946c39" :question/math-academy-id "e-8235"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575544}] :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "e4fabb97-fb83-54ac-bf48-59a6c3ed7682" :step/math-academy-id 30811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083323 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083125 :question/id #uuid "c4f84572-0883-5fb5-b5d8-513d1aa2f50a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8236"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01100ea8-2ab9-511e-8321-6ef5588c4417" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050926}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575609}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations to Compute an Unknown Mean or Standard Deviation"} :step/id #uuid "83423fd5-cc32-5942-8633-6f6c285f43c7" :step/math-academy-id 30812 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084267}}] :activity/title "The Z-Score" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083415}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575690} :activity/id #uuid "81f06c37-0e57-589d-ba7c-883c5c4cad26" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048340 :topic/id #uuid "97021ff6-453f-5638-924e-46e41b2ed833" :topic/math-academy-id 711} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d31a8c1-021c-5a4c-876f-09f1568ff61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575147} :answer-field/id #uuid "332a9a32-3521-5a06-a046-98035661faf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e17b593f-806a-5407-8b55-7558d69aa888" :question/math-academy-id "q-332244" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.1,{x}_{2}=7.9$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.5$ and ${z}_{2}=1.5,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation $\\sigma$ of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.1=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n7.9=\\overset{¯}{x}+1.5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.9-3.1 & =(1.5-(-0.5))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4.8 & =2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "18887134-da9f-5012-b460-09608a69231b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a8a690-0e17-513a-9759-e2c32710072c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302862} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c1b1da-a3d8-5b1d-9536-eeb4e916f1d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbfb0339-7153-551d-aafb-c429f4a2a68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332239" :question/problem "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.08,{x}_{2}=8.68$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-1.2$ and ${z}_{2}=2.3,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.08=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n8.68=\\overset{¯}{x}+2.3\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.68-3.08 & =(2.3-(-1.2))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.6 & =3.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.92 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e8e4cf2-3411-5582-a3cd-7656d38811f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6137d563-5854-54cb-a38e-1055473864b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575123} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5c354f-b3fb-557e-b2a1-3bac47f270b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50d225de-7004-5810-a19e-8625e7e461c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-332028" :question/problem "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma =2.4$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=5.4$ and $z$-score $z=0.5.$ Find the mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{―}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.4,$ $z=0.5,$ and $\\sigma =2.4$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+0.5\\cdot 2.4 \\\\ 5.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+1.2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ad4fae5-16c5-5953-9a43-8d68164e0aee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a53cb31-bc17-5daf-8994-cbb6560d170a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575213} {:answer/id #uuid "ae404e3a-dbf6-5282-b23e-6e487d6722fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575260} {:answer/id #uuid "c3652fcb-627b-5e2d-87c4-3c8135bc864b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575295} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d16dea-91d4-5e65-88b9-96458855ddc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575319} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b2b3cc-8ebe-5843-8f10-433a90a3310c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575295} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3433b4b-8883-5522-b986-cd5716b0db79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af87d523-ac60-5aec-8cf6-a5f255a961ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-94473" :question/problem "A data set has mean $\\overset{¯}{x}=16$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =2.5.$ Find the value of an observation that has $z$-score $z=-0.4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{¯}{x}=16,$ $z=-0.4,$ and $\\sigma =2.5$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =16+(-0.4)\\cdot 2.5 \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8eba139a-1a0c-507d-b6c4-a5a5034f9011" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575666}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad868d2-4b59-59e5-af41-cdcaa965a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575049} {:answer/id #uuid "526b6aa7-3f19-53ef-a532-7f9286139f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575092} {:answer/id #uuid "b75010ed-05a2-5fae-94e7-e38cb062d94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575275} {:answer/id #uuid "d87a2113-3ca9-5e90-9173-5ef22a26472f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575338} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6c033b-6092-5094-8efd-03c857a491a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575049} :answer-field/id #uuid "df0001ec-e44a-52b2-ad04-aa3230b4f04d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "543c2585-7af9-533a-a269-b37c45eeb579" :question/math-academy-id "q-9787" :question/problem "The owner of a restaurant noted that $145$ vegetarian customers visited his restaurant during a particular week. On a particular day of the same week, he noted that $x=15$ vegetarian customers visited the restaurant, corresponding to a $z$-score of $z=-1.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the number of vegetarian customers per day, giving your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the mean number of vegetarian customers for each day:\n\n$$\n\\overset{¯}{x}=\\frac{145}{7}=20.71\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set. Rewriting this in terms of $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{z}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard deviation $\\sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sigma  & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-20.71}{-1.5} \\\\  & =3.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "fcc6ad5b-0190-5e5b-a8a1-c87fcea55ab0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575669}}] :activity/title "The Z-Score" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574942}]}
